---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout>
  <section class="hero hero-with-portrait wrap" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">Customer perspective. Engineering depth.</p>
    <h1 id="hero-title">Senior Solutions<br /><span class="hero-title-secondary">Architect</span><span style="color:var(--brass)">.</span></h1>
    <p class="intro">I turn complex technical requirements into solutions customers, partners, and engineering teams can act on.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="#work">Explore my work <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="text-link" href="/resume/">View résumé <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    </div>
    <figure class="hero-portrait"><img src="/images/james-delosh-portrait.webp" width="1200" height="806" alt="James Delosh" fetchpriority="high" /></figure>
    <div class="hero-foot"><span>Platform integrations · Solutions engineering · Hands-on delivery</span><span>From discovery to a working solution</span></div>
  </section>
  <section id="work" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="work-title">
    <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / Selected work</p><h2 id="work-title">Ideas made concrete.</h2></div><p>A working product, a reference architecture, and hands-on integrations—from security workflows to personal automation.</p></div>
    <article class="feature"><div class="feature-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">Featured project / Product design & development</p><h3>Skeet Tracker</h3><div class="feature-meta"><span>Product ownership</span><span>Data modeling</span><span>Cloudflare</span></div></div><div><p>Competitive shooting produces plenty of scores. Understanding your progress takes more work. I built Skeet Tracker to bring tournament history, rolling averages, and classifications into one usable view.</p><div class="project-links"><a class="text-link" href="/work/skeet-tracker/">Read case study →</a><a class="text-link" href="https://sk33t.net">Live app ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/jtdelosh-ops/Skeet-Tracker">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></div><figure class="screenshot-stage"><div class="screenshot-frame"><div class="window-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="window-dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span><span>sk33t.net</span><span>↗</span></div><img src="/images/skeet-tracker.webp" srcset="/images/skeet-tracker-small.webp 700w, /images/skeet-tracker.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width:720px) calc(100vw - 72px), 1100px" width="1200" height="960" alt="Skeet Tracker showing gauge averages, HOA and HAA totals, and tournament history" fetchpriority="high" /></div><figcaption class="feature-caption">A real product, built around the details of a sport I know firsthand.</figcaption></figure></article>
    <div class="work-grid"><article class="project-card"><p class="eyebrow">Solution design / Independent case study</p><h3>Embedded Workforce<br />Platform Integration</h3><p>A reference architecture for bringing an employment platform into a partner’s product, with clear ownership and recoverable workflows.</p><div class="project-visual system-strip" aria-label="Partner platform connects through an integration service to an employment platform"><span>Partner<br />Platform</span><b aria-hidden="true">↔</b><span>Integration<br />Service</span><b aria-hidden="true">↔</b><span>Employment<br />Platform</span></div><p class="disclaimer">An independent exercise using fictional platforms. Not an internal or production system.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/embedded-workforce/">Explore the architecture →</a></article><article class="project-card"><p class="eyebrow">Security automation / Independent interview project</p><h3>Security Workflow Automation</h3><p>Two n8n workflows connecting phishing and identity alerts to enrichment, response decisions, and analyst notifications.</p><a class="project-visual" href="/work/security-automation/" aria-label="Explore Security Workflow Automation"><img src="/images/phishing-triage-workflow.webp" width="1050" height="340" loading="lazy" alt="Phishing triage workflow with enrichment and manual-review branches" /></a><p class="disclaimer">Built with sandbox integrations and simulated alerts for a Torq interview.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/security-automation/">Explore workflows & demo →</a></article></div><article class="project-card riker-compact"><p class="eyebrow">Automation / AI integration</p><h3>Riker Bot</h3><p>A Python Discord bot with scheduled posts, interactive commands, and optional AI advice. A playful project with deliberate controls for timing, access, cost, and recovery.</p><div class="project-visual bot-commands" aria-label="Selected Riker Bot commands"><code>/riker quote</code><code>/riker advice</code><code>/riker status</code><code>/riker test_auto</code></div><p style="margin-bottom:24px">Timezone-aware scheduling. Persistent state. Useful diagnostics.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/riker-bot/">View project →</a></article>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="approach-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / How I work</p><h2 id="approach-title">Start with the problem.<br />Make the path clear.</h2></div></div><div class="steps"><article><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Discover</h3><p>Understand the customer’s goals, the people involved, and the constraints that will shape a useful solution.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Design</h3><p>Define system boundaries, data ownership, and tradeoffs. Account for what happens when the happy path ends.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Explain</h3><p>Use diagrams, demonstrations, and clear language to connect engineering decisions to business outcomes.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">04</span><h3>Prove</h3><p>Test the important assumptions through hands-on integrations, focused proofs of concept, and technical validation.</p></article></div></section>
  <section id="experience" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="experience-title"><div class="experience-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / Experience</p><h2 id="experience-title">Technical depth.<br />Customer context.</h2><p class="lead">My career spans security operations, customer-facing engineering, and platform architecture. The through line: understand the problem, work through the details, and help people move forward.</p><a class="text-link" href="/resume/">Full experience & résumé →</a></div><div><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">2026 — Present</p><div><h3>Remote</h3><p class="role">Senior Partner Solutions Architect,<br />Platform Partnerships</p><p class="summary">Translate strategic partner requirements into integration architectures, working across APIs, OAuth, webhooks, SDKs, and embedded experiences with partner and internal engineering teams.</p></div></article><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">2021 — 2025</p><div><h3>RangeForce · SCYTHE · Hunters</h3><p class="role">Solutions & sales engineering / Adversary emulation</p><p class="summary">Led discovery, demonstrations, and technical evaluations. Helped customers validate security workflows, troubleshoot integrations, and connect product capabilities to their environment.</p></div></article><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">2018 — 2021</p><div><h3>Verodin · JPMorgan Chase</h3><p class="role">Sales Engineer → Consulting Engineer</p><p class="summary">Progressed from controls-validation PoCs and executive readouts at Verodin to a Consulting Engineer engagement at JPMorgan Chase as a full-time contractor on behalf of Verodin.</p></div></article><article class="timeline-item"><p class="date">2017 — 2018</p><div><h3>SAS · IBM Resilient</h3><p class="role">Partner enablement / Enterprise sales engineering</p><p class="summary">Supported partner deployments, SOAR evaluations, and Python integrations connecting endpoint detection, incident response, and automated action.</p></div></article><p class="earlier">Earlier: Dell EMC security operations and leadership in the United States Marine Corps.</p></div></div></section>
  <section id="beyond" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="beyond-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / Beyond work</p><h2 id="beyond-title">Away from the screen.</h2></div><p>Usually on a skeet field or finding a reason to get back underwater.</p></div><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="photo-card"><img src="/images/skeet-rain.webp" width="510" height="680" loading="lazy" alt="James skeet shooting outdoors in the rain" /><figcaption><span class="photo-label">On the skeet field</span><h3>Some days, the weather wins.</h3><p>Skeet shooting is part of my life outside work—and the reason Skeet Tracker exists. Rain doesn’t always change the plan.</p></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo-card"><img src="/images/scuba-100.webp" srcset="/images/scuba-100-small.webp 700w, /images/scuba-100.webp 1440w" sizes="(max-width:720px) calc(100vw - 40px), 650px" width="1440" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="James underwater celebrating his 100th scuba dive" /><figcaption><span class="photo-label">Below the surface</span><h3>One hundred dives. Still curious.</h3><p>A milestone from my 100th dive. There’s always something else to see when you slow down and look around.</p></figcaption></figure></div></section>
</Layout>
